1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,160
Shut up! There's no way! She so did not do that.

2
00:00:06,540 --> 00:00:07,640
Oh, yes, she did.

3
00:00:09,380 --> 00:00:09,940
Yes!

4
00:00:12,060 --> 00:00:14,700
Hurry up! We're gonna get in trouble if we're late again.

5
00:00:14,820 --> 00:00:15,660
No, I'm trying.

6
00:00:17,640 --> 00:00:19,580
So, you went ahead and bought them?

7
00:00:20,020 --> 00:00:23,020
Yep, brand new shoes. I'm gonna be super fast today.

8
00:00:23,240 --> 00:00:25,940
Just you wait. You'll be eating my dust.

9
00:00:26,140 --> 00:00:26,860
Oh, whatever.

10
00:00:26,860 --> 00:00:31,360
Miss Takeyama, please return to the faculty room as soon as possible.

11
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:34,280
Yeah, that one made me cry a lot, too.

12
00:00:35,820 --> 00:00:37,140
Why is he in the library?

13
00:00:37,520 --> 00:00:39,000
You girls are late again.

14
00:00:39,540 --> 00:00:40,140
We're sorry.

15
00:00:40,560 --> 00:00:42,820
Get changed immediately and start running laps.

16
00:00:42,880 --> 00:00:43,220
Okay.

17
00:00:43,860 --> 00:00:45,040
And get a better attitude.

18
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:45,760
Okay.

19
00:00:46,720 --> 00:00:48,540
So, Senna, where are you going now?

20
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:51,620
I did bring that movie I was talking about if you wanted to see it.

21
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:55,840
What's wrong, Senna?

22
00:00:56,860 --> 00:01:09,300
I forgot my gym clothes.

23
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:13,540
Do you want to go back to that same store again?

24
00:01:13,700 --> 00:01:15,500
I was just about to ask you the same question.

25
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:26,780
Oh, my God, we almost died.

26
00:01:26,780 --> 00:01:27,360
He is totally the worst.

27
00:01:27,360 --> 00:01:29,580
Okay, then let's do it last.

28
00:01:29,580 --> 00:01:31,580
You

29
00:03:01,806 --> 00:03:07,726
Kyo, you're so exaggerating.

30
00:03:07,766 --> 00:03:08,786
I'm totally not.

31
00:03:08,886 --> 00:03:11,566
I'm telling you, they both almost died, like, right there in the hallway.

32
00:03:16,046 --> 00:03:17,646
Um, no, it's not.

33
00:03:17,766 --> 00:03:18,326
Like, at all.

34
00:03:18,346 --> 00:03:19,206
Who's in there talking?

35
00:03:19,746 --> 00:03:21,566
She must be on a cell phone or something.

36
00:03:22,386 --> 00:03:24,246
Come on, you always say that.

37
00:03:25,926 --> 00:03:26,926
I know that girl.

38
00:03:27,026 --> 00:03:28,166
How many times do I have to tell you?

39
00:03:28,306 --> 00:03:29,306
It never happened.

40
00:03:30,046 --> 00:03:30,846
No, it didn't.

41
00:03:30,846 --> 00:03:32,246
She's in my class.

42
00:03:33,006 --> 00:03:33,806
Yozara something.

43
00:03:34,546 --> 00:03:35,566
Mikazuki, I think.

44
00:03:36,066 --> 00:03:38,966
But I've never heard her laugh like that in class.

45
00:03:39,286 --> 00:03:40,386
Or, well, ever.

46
00:03:41,346 --> 00:03:43,206
She always looks pissed as hell.

47
00:03:43,706 --> 00:03:45,546
Kind of like she's about to murder someone.

48
00:03:46,486 --> 00:03:48,726
I've never really seen her talk to anybody.

49
00:03:49,226 --> 00:03:49,746
Crazy.

50
00:03:50,626 --> 00:03:52,526
Hold on, this is officially weird.

51
00:03:52,946 --> 00:03:54,486
She's not talking on the phone at all.

52
00:03:54,786 --> 00:03:56,706
I told you, I'm not giving up on that.

53
00:03:58,506 --> 00:03:59,466
What the hell?

54
00:03:59,466 --> 00:04:01,126
Well, who's she talking to?

55
00:04:01,566 --> 00:04:03,006
Is there somebody hiding in there?

56
00:04:03,886 --> 00:04:04,906
So why not?

57
00:04:05,066 --> 00:04:08,506
A girl talking to an invisible thing in an empty classroom at dusk.

58
00:04:09,166 --> 00:04:11,026
But you can't just sit there and wait for it.

59
00:04:11,106 --> 00:04:14,786
You know, this sounds exactly like the beginning of a book I just started reading.

60
00:04:14,786 --> 00:04:23,586
Next, after various obstacles, I discover her secret and ultimately defend her by fighting and killing a thing not of this world.

61
00:04:24,746 --> 00:04:27,966
Then we would naturally, uh, fall in love.

62
00:04:29,246 --> 00:04:29,986
I guess.

63
00:04:30,126 --> 00:04:30,366
All right.

64
00:04:30,366 --> 00:04:32,006
You know what, Tomo?

65
00:04:32,006 --> 00:04:33,386
I think I do remember that.

66
00:04:34,286 --> 00:04:34,626
Huh?

67
00:04:34,626 --> 00:05:00,686
That's what she usually looks like.

68
00:05:00,686 --> 00:05:03,966
But she was so cute when she was laughing.

69
00:05:05,186 --> 00:05:06,286
What do you want?

70
00:05:07,146 --> 00:05:11,346
I, I was just wondering, do you like see ghosts and stuff?

71
00:05:11,666 --> 00:05:12,066
What?

72
00:05:12,586 --> 00:05:14,846
Uh, no, cause ghosts don't exist.

73
00:05:15,266 --> 00:05:17,166
Well then, who have you been talking to?

74
00:05:17,286 --> 00:05:18,206
You were watching me?

75
00:05:18,206 --> 00:05:30,146
Look, I was having a conversation with my best friend ever.

76
00:05:30,526 --> 00:05:31,706
She's made of air, okay?

77
00:05:41,446 --> 00:05:42,726
You have an air friend?

78
00:05:43,366 --> 00:05:44,566
What the crap is that?

79
00:05:44,566 --> 00:05:47,326
It's exactly what I said it was.

80
00:05:47,646 --> 00:05:49,466
You've heard of an air guitar before, haven't you?

81
00:05:49,886 --> 00:05:51,206
She's like that, but cooler.

82
00:05:51,886 --> 00:05:56,126
So let's see, you pretend someone is there and talk to them like they're talking back to you?

83
00:05:56,306 --> 00:05:57,126
I'm not pretending.

84
00:05:57,846 --> 00:05:59,186
Tomo exists, you big jerk.

85
00:05:59,686 --> 00:06:01,046
See, she's right here next to me.

86
00:06:01,766 --> 00:06:06,866
For example, Tomo and I were just talking about this one time when we went to a theme park back when we were in middle school.

87
00:06:07,146 --> 00:06:09,726
This group of guys was totally hitting on us while we were there.

88
00:06:09,726 --> 00:06:13,766
I mean, it was ridiculous, so I decided to pretend one of them was going to be our hot new home room teacher.

89
00:06:13,766 --> 00:06:15,066
You just said you were pretending.

90
00:06:15,246 --> 00:06:15,786
Shut up.

91
00:06:15,986 --> 00:06:17,226
The point is, it really happened.

92
00:06:18,886 --> 00:06:19,646
Oh, really?

93
00:06:20,386 --> 00:06:21,846
How much of it really happened?

94
00:06:22,186 --> 00:06:23,646
The part where I was in middle school?

95
00:06:23,746 --> 00:06:25,986
So only 99% of it was a complete lie?

96
00:06:26,326 --> 00:06:28,986
I was hoping the amusement park would at least turn out to be real.

97
00:06:29,526 --> 00:06:29,886
Seriously?

98
00:06:30,126 --> 00:06:32,206
Who would want to go to an amusement park by themselves?

99
00:06:32,226 --> 00:06:33,586
You just admitted you were alone.

100
00:06:33,766 --> 00:06:34,126
Stop.

101
00:06:34,426 --> 00:06:37,466
Go ahead and get over it, because Tomo is unbelievably adorable.

102
00:06:37,466 --> 00:06:39,126
That chick is off a rocker.

103
00:06:39,566 --> 00:06:40,506
This cannot end well.

104
00:06:40,846 --> 00:06:42,866
So this is really about self-preservation.

105
00:06:43,426 --> 00:06:45,186
What does that look for?

106
00:06:45,606 --> 00:06:46,226
I don't know.

107
00:06:46,766 --> 00:06:50,346
Wouldn't it be a lot easier for you to just, you know, make a real friend instead?

108
00:06:50,906 --> 00:06:54,826
Not an air friend like adorable Tomo, but an actual living person.

109
00:06:55,986 --> 00:06:58,146
Yeah, well, I would if I could, thank you.

110
00:06:59,206 --> 00:07:00,546
Shocking that you can't.

111
00:07:02,626 --> 00:07:04,386
And just who are you to talk?

112
00:07:04,386 --> 00:07:07,266
You're that new kid everyone's afraid of, isn't that right?

113
00:07:07,466 --> 00:07:09,766
I've been at this school for well over a month now.

114
00:07:10,086 --> 00:07:11,306
I'm hardly a new kid.

115
00:07:11,546 --> 00:07:12,426
What's your name?

116
00:07:13,406 --> 00:07:15,026
It's Kodoka Hasegawa.

117
00:07:16,386 --> 00:07:20,166
It seems to me you have no right to say anything about having friends, Kodoka.

118
00:07:20,406 --> 00:07:21,906
First name basis already?

119
00:07:22,146 --> 00:07:25,446
So an entire month has passed and you still haven't made a single friend yet?

120
00:07:25,646 --> 00:07:27,086
Wouldn't you say that's a little pathetic?

121
00:07:27,406 --> 00:07:29,466
Hey, you only have an air friend.

122
00:07:29,546 --> 00:07:30,726
Where the hell do you get off?

123
00:07:30,766 --> 00:07:32,446
There's no reason to be rude to her.

124
00:07:32,446 --> 00:07:35,246
My friend Tomo is adorable and crazy smart.

125
00:07:35,246 --> 00:07:39,766
And she's also an amazing athlete and the absolute best friend a girl like me could ever hope to have.

126
00:07:40,286 --> 00:07:45,226
What's more, she would never even dream about stabbing me in the back.

127
00:07:45,226 --> 00:07:51,206
It's not a mystery.

128
00:07:51,986 --> 00:07:53,286
There's nothing else I can do.

129
00:07:54,026 --> 00:07:55,546
I don't know how to make friends.

130
00:07:58,106 --> 00:08:00,166
Yeah, I'm not really that great at it either.

131
00:08:00,926 --> 00:08:02,926
Did you not have any friends at your old school?

132
00:08:03,386 --> 00:08:04,246
Of course I did.

133
00:08:04,726 --> 00:08:05,926
Don't you still talk to them?

134
00:08:06,526 --> 00:08:08,906
He said he would text me, but I haven't heard from him.

135
00:08:09,446 --> 00:08:12,106
Maybe you misunderstood and just, like, thought you were friends.

136
00:08:12,606 --> 00:08:13,606
Maybe shut up.

137
00:08:13,606 --> 00:08:16,566
Fine, then.

138
00:08:17,046 --> 00:08:19,986
You know, if you wanted, you could try to pay someone to be friends with you.

139
00:08:20,386 --> 00:08:21,726
No, that's so lame.

140
00:08:21,886 --> 00:08:22,646
Come on.

141
00:08:22,906 --> 00:08:25,666
A small stipend every week for spending time together at school.

142
00:08:26,446 --> 00:08:27,826
Like a prostitute?

143
00:08:28,406 --> 00:08:29,906
What is that, a friendstitute?

144
00:08:30,406 --> 00:08:31,946
No, it's a basic contract.

145
00:08:32,966 --> 00:08:35,106
Yeah, there's nothing wrong with a contract.

146
00:08:35,446 --> 00:08:37,726
Or you could just ask someone to be your friend.

147
00:08:39,346 --> 00:08:42,786
I've seen people do that on after-school specials, but I don't really get it.

148
00:08:43,606 --> 00:08:48,086
You ask someone to be friends, and the moment they agree, a friendship is suddenly formed.

149
00:08:48,726 --> 00:08:50,786
Even if you'd never spoken to each other before then.

150
00:08:51,406 --> 00:08:53,566
What if you never even talk after getting approval?

151
00:08:53,986 --> 00:08:56,166
Can you still consider that person to be your friend?

152
00:08:56,946 --> 00:08:57,346
Congrats!

153
00:08:57,586 --> 00:08:57,966
Awesome!

154
00:08:57,966 --> 00:09:00,846
Yeah, well, I guess I can't really blame you.

155
00:09:01,086 --> 00:09:02,286
It's not my thing, either.

156
00:09:02,926 --> 00:09:06,186
Besides, I'm pretty independent, so it's not like I need to have any friends.

157
00:09:06,526 --> 00:09:06,966
Right.

158
00:09:08,326 --> 00:09:10,966
Not having friends must be so hard on that poor girl.

159
00:09:11,606 --> 00:09:14,226
Condescension has to be my least favorite thing in the whole world.

160
00:09:14,826 --> 00:09:16,806
Well, I can understand.

161
00:09:17,486 --> 00:09:21,986
For whatever reason, people think if you don't have a lot of friends, there's something wrong with you.

162
00:09:21,986 --> 00:09:24,066
I don't think that's true, though.

163
00:09:24,706 --> 00:09:26,126
At least I hope it's not.

164
00:09:26,666 --> 00:09:28,426
I'm perfectly fine being alone.

165
00:09:28,926 --> 00:09:33,406
Having a few general acquaintances at school, those types of superficial friends are enough for me.

166
00:09:34,086 --> 00:09:38,286
Yeah, but still, I have to admit, it would be nice to have a real friend.

167
00:09:38,286 --> 00:09:46,266
What about school clubs?

168
00:09:46,566 --> 00:09:47,086
No way.

169
00:09:47,426 --> 00:09:48,466
Why is that a bad idea?

170
00:09:48,946 --> 00:09:50,326
Because we're in our second year.

171
00:09:51,426 --> 00:09:53,366
Club-based friendships have already been established.

172
00:09:54,746 --> 00:09:58,706
It'd be too embarrassing to join one now, not to mention completely obvious.

173
00:09:59,186 --> 00:10:00,166
I guess that's true.

174
00:10:01,166 --> 00:10:04,826
If you're only joining a club to make friends and not because you share an interest,

175
00:10:05,046 --> 00:10:06,626
you're only going to screw with their dynamic.

176
00:10:06,626 --> 00:10:09,846
You'll end up being the one loser member with nothing to offer.

177
00:10:10,326 --> 00:10:11,806
Why would they even want you to join?

178
00:10:13,126 --> 00:10:14,146
She's got a point.

179
00:10:14,586 --> 00:10:16,326
I'd only join a club to meet people.

180
00:10:16,526 --> 00:10:17,886
I don't really have any other reason.

181
00:10:18,346 --> 00:10:20,466
And I would hate to bug them if they were already friends.

182
00:10:20,786 --> 00:10:21,706
That would suck.

183
00:10:28,666 --> 00:10:29,566
That's perfect.

184
00:10:29,906 --> 00:10:30,766
A club!

185
00:10:31,206 --> 00:10:31,546
Huh?

186
00:10:36,626 --> 00:10:38,606
See you later, Kodaka.

187
00:10:38,606 --> 00:10:39,586
Hmm.

188
00:10:39,586 --> 00:11:01,426
Why my hair looks like this?

189
00:11:01,426 --> 00:11:05,326
Oh, well, my late mother was English, so I get it from her.

190
00:11:06,106 --> 00:11:09,226
I mean, hey, it's not really PC, but have at it.

191
00:11:09,546 --> 00:11:10,066
Thank you.

192
00:11:10,646 --> 00:11:13,586
It was pretty difficult, but I guess time heals all wounds, right?

193
00:11:13,586 --> 00:11:18,086
My father decided to go abroad for work.

194
00:11:18,386 --> 00:11:21,126
I started going to St. Chronica's Academy in Toyashi then.

195
00:11:21,506 --> 00:11:25,466
But my first day of school, I took the wrong bus and ended up getting there really late.

196
00:11:27,806 --> 00:11:31,746
When I finally arrived at the academy, I was on the verge of tears.

197
00:11:34,086 --> 00:11:36,586
Compared to crusading armies, Saladin's army...

198
00:11:36,586 --> 00:11:41,086
Hello.

199
00:11:41,086 --> 00:11:41,206
Hello.

200
00:11:42,366 --> 00:11:44,546
I'm, uh, the transfer student.

201
00:11:45,126 --> 00:11:46,306
Okay, great.

202
00:11:46,586 --> 00:11:47,646
My name is Kodaka.

203
00:11:48,506 --> 00:11:49,966
Nice to be here.

204
00:11:50,426 --> 00:11:53,366
Oh, no.

205
00:11:53,586 --> 00:11:54,226
Ms. Osada.

206
00:11:54,586 --> 00:11:55,786
Did he just attack her?

207
00:11:55,946 --> 00:11:57,026
Delinquent much?

208
00:11:57,146 --> 00:11:57,986
She's new here.

209
00:11:58,006 --> 00:11:59,026
Want some of his hair?

210
00:11:59,026 --> 00:12:12,286
Oh, I forgot my book.

211
00:12:15,166 --> 00:12:16,566
Here, take mine.

212
00:12:17,146 --> 00:12:18,446
Oh, thanks.

213
00:12:21,026 --> 00:12:25,606
By the evening of my first day, the whole school had heard the rumor.

214
00:12:25,606 --> 00:12:29,846
I was a thug who stole the book of the poor girl sitting next to me.

215
00:12:35,566 --> 00:12:37,046
It's been a month since then,

216
00:12:37,146 --> 00:12:40,746
and I still haven't been able to redeem myself for that first day fail.

217
00:12:41,746 --> 00:12:42,926
See you later, Kodaka.

218
00:12:44,886 --> 00:12:48,366
What a weird girl.

219
00:12:49,546 --> 00:12:51,586
I did like that she said my first name.

220
00:12:51,646 --> 00:12:53,006
I haven't heard that at school yet.

221
00:12:53,006 --> 00:12:55,586
Nice.

222
00:12:56,966 --> 00:12:59,126
In equations of the nth degree,

223
00:12:59,546 --> 00:13:00,726
there will always be a root.

224
00:13:01,126 --> 00:13:02,966
As a result, the root of n...

225
00:13:02,966 --> 00:13:05,946
The usual.

226
00:13:08,746 --> 00:13:10,086
What's wrong with her now?

227
00:13:15,626 --> 00:13:17,666
Kodaka, follow me, please.

228
00:13:17,966 --> 00:13:19,346
Holy crap, she talked to it.

229
00:13:19,366 --> 00:13:20,586
To that Hasekawa creep?

230
00:13:20,586 --> 00:13:22,986
Oh, well, give me a second.

231
00:13:25,306 --> 00:13:26,626
Okay, what's going on?

232
00:13:26,966 --> 00:13:28,766
I've gone through all the formalities and crap.

233
00:13:29,126 --> 00:13:30,506
What are you talking about?

234
00:13:30,646 --> 00:13:31,786
I created a new club.

235
00:13:32,146 --> 00:13:34,186
Since we can't join a club that already exists,

236
00:13:34,546 --> 00:13:37,666
well, it seemed only logical for me to go make a new one.

237
00:13:38,226 --> 00:13:39,206
Yeah, I get it.

238
00:13:39,426 --> 00:13:42,886
In a new club, you don't have to stress out about making things awkward for anybody.

239
00:13:42,886 --> 00:13:46,126
So what kind of club did you end up making?

240
00:13:53,466 --> 00:13:55,026
The Neighbors Club.

241
00:13:55,666 --> 00:13:56,946
The Neighbors Club?

242
00:13:57,506 --> 00:13:58,626
That's right.

243
00:13:58,626 --> 00:14:04,086
In compliance with God's instructions, we embrace everyone as neighbors,

244
00:14:04,586 --> 00:14:07,286
deepen friendships on a spiritual level, yada, yada, yada,

245
00:14:07,286 --> 00:14:11,126
work together and treat everyone with the kindness that God would want or whatnot.

246
00:14:11,966 --> 00:14:14,266
Will a club like that actually get approved, though?

247
00:14:14,486 --> 00:14:15,526
Are you kidding me?

248
00:14:15,646 --> 00:14:17,126
This school is full of suckers.

249
00:14:17,506 --> 00:14:18,946
Throw in God or working hard?

250
00:14:18,946 --> 00:14:20,806
They cannot get enough of that stuff.

251
00:14:21,206 --> 00:14:22,946
Religion is always an easy sell.

252
00:14:23,406 --> 00:14:26,726
All right, so then what's this Neighbors Club actually going to do?

253
00:14:27,026 --> 00:14:29,106
It's going to help us make some damn friends.

254
00:14:29,426 --> 00:14:33,186
And we can avoid getting a bunch of pity fates from the other students while we're at it.

255
00:14:33,446 --> 00:14:36,066
We'll load up on as many shallow acquaintances as we can

256
00:14:36,066 --> 00:14:39,826
and then keep our eye out for people we could tolerate as real friends.

257
00:14:40,066 --> 00:14:41,766
Interesting plan of attack, I guess.

258
00:14:42,186 --> 00:14:43,326
Well, good luck.

259
